  "style": "",
  "text": "Another possible contributor to the high prevalence could have been inadequate breastfeeding. The reduced duration of breastfeeding has been shown to increase risk of childhood infections, some of which increase the risk of anemia [15]. Also inadequate breastfeeding increases the risk of malnutrition [11], which in turn increases risk of anemia as explained in the previous paragraphs. Consistent with this, Nabunya et al. (2020) found that only 42.8% of mothers working in the informal sector in Kampala, Uganda, exclusively breastfed their infants below six months of age, and that proper breastfeeding practices were associated with a four-fold higher prevalence of exclusive breastfeeding (adjusted prevalence ratio [APR] 4.12; 95% CI 2.88–5.90) [11]."
}

