  "style": "",
  "text": "Regarding factors, children whose caretakers had a lower education level were 3.204 times more likely to have anemia, and those with more than four siblings were 3.428 times more likely to have anemia. Both caretaker education level and the number of siblings are indicators of socioeconomic status. In most Ugandan settings, lower education is associated with lower income, while a high number of siblings is likewise often associated with poor socioeconomic status: the limited resources available must be divided among many children, which results in poorer feeding and care for each child and, ultimately, in undernutrition and anemia [24, 25]."
}

